---
import ToolLayout from '../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import JsonDiffTool from '../components/tools/json/JsonDiffTool';

const relatedTools = ['json-diff', 'json-formatter', 'json-validator', 'base64-tool'];

const faqSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How does the online JSON Diff tool compare two JSON files?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "The tool parses both JSON inputs, normalizes and formats the keys into a standard 2-space indented structure, and runs a high-speed line-by-line comparison algorithm to highlight additions in green, deletions in red, and modifications inline."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Is my JSON data secure and private?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Yes. All diff operations and string comparisons run 100% client-side in your browser's local sandbox using the V8 JavaScript engine. No payload data, API tokens, or webhooks are transmitted over the network."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is the difference between Unified and Side-by-Side diff views?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Unified view stacks deletions and additions sequentially in a single scrolling pane, which is useful for small payloads. Side-by-side view renders original and modified objects horizontally across two columns, mirroring a standard Git Pull Request review interface."
      }
    }
  ]
};
---

<ToolLayout
  title="JSON Diff Online — Fast, Private JSON Compare Tool"
  description="Compare two JSON objects side-by-side or unified. Free, private, in-browser JSON diff tool with syntax auto-formatting and zero data tracking."
  toolName="JSON Diff"
  category="Developer"
  relatedToolIds={relatedTools}
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqSchema)} />

  <JsonDiffTool client:load />

  <article class="mt-16 border-t border-black/10 dark:border-white/10 pt-10 text-neutral-800 dark:text-neutral-200 space-y-8 text-xs leading-relaxed">
    
    <header class="space-y-3">
      <h2 class="text-base font-bold text-neutral-900 dark:text-white">
        Online JSON Diff & Comparison Utility
      </h2>
      <p class="text-neutral-600 dark:text-neutral-400 text-[13px] leading-loose">
        When developing REST APIs, managing microservices, or debugging complex infrastructure configuration files, identifying subtle schema variations between environments is critical. A misplaced bracket, an altered boolean, or a missing nested array can cause catastrophic application failures. This JSON Diff tool provides a fast, zero-latency visual comparator that pinpoints field deletions, added properties, and modified values instantly. By automatically normalizing raw, minified, or messy data structures prior to comparison, developers can immediately spot the semantic differences without fighting formatting inconsistencies.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-6">
      <div class="p-5 rounded-xl bg-neutral-100/70 dark:bg-neutral-900/60 border border-black/5 dark:border-white/5 space-y-2">
        <span class="font-bold text-neutral-900 dark:text-white block text-sm">1. Paste JSON Payloads</span>
        <p class="text-neutral-600 dark:text-neutral-400">Input your original JSON on the left panel and the modified version on the right. Both raw stringified payloads and heavily minified formats are accepted.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="p-5 rounded-xl bg-neutral-100/70 dark:bg-neutral-900/60 border border-black/5 dark:border-white/5 space-y-2">
        <span class="font-bold text-neutral-900 dark:text-white block text-sm">2. Automatic Prettifying</span>
        <p class="text-neutral-600 dark:text-neutral-400">Before executing the diff, the engine safely parses and auto-formats messy JSON strings into consistent, readable 2-space indented trees to ensure accurate line mapping.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="p-5 rounded-xl bg-neutral-100/70 dark:bg-neutral-900/60 border border-black/5 dark:border-white/5 space-y-2">
        <span class="font-bold text-neutral-900 dark:text-white block text-sm">3. Inspect Visual Diff</span>
        <p class="text-neutral-600 dark:text-neutral-400">Toggle seamlessly between Git PR-style Side-by-Side inspection or a single-column Unified diff mode to review every highlighted insertion and deletion.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <section class="space-y-4">
      <h3 class="text-sm font-bold text-neutral-900 dark:text-white">
        Common Developer Use Cases for JSON Diffing
      </h3>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-neutral-600 dark:text-neutral-400 text-[13px] leading-loose">
        <li><strong>API Payload Debugging:</strong> Compare a successful 200 OK webhook response against a failing 400 Bad Request payload to isolate missing fields or incorrect data types.</li>
        <li><strong>Configuration File Auditing:</strong> Review changes made to a <code>package.json</code>, <code>tsconfig.json</code>, or Docker configuration file before committing them to version control.</li>
        <li><strong>Cloud Infrastructure Policies:</strong> Validate complex nested AWS IAM policies or Google Cloud resource definitions to ensure no unintended permissions were granted during an update.</li>
        <li><strong>Database Record Inspection:</strong> Compare document-oriented database records (like MongoDB or DynamoDB exports) to track state changes over time.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-3">
      <h3 class="text-sm font-bold text-neutral-900 dark:text-white">
        Why Compare JSON Client-Side?
      </h3>
      <p class="text-neutral-600 dark:text-neutral-400 text-[13px] leading-loose">
        Traditional online diff checkers transmit your pasted data to remote web servers for backend processing. When working with authentication tokens, production API payloads, proprietary business logic, or customer database snapshots, sharing unencrypted data creates unnecessary security exposure and violates compliance protocols like GDPR or HIPAA. 
      </p>
      <p class="text-neutral-600 dark:text-neutral-400 text-[13px] leading-loose">
        SiteOfTools executes all diffing algorithms in memory entirely inside your device's browser sandbox. Your strings, payloads, and files are never logged, cached, or transmitted over external networks. Once you close the tab, the data is gone forever, providing enterprise-grade privacy for sensitive comparisons.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-4 border-t border-black/5 dark:border-white/5 pt-8">
      <h3 class="text-sm font-bold text-neutral-900 dark:text-white">
        Frequently Asked Questions
      </h3>
      <dl class="space-y-4">
        <div class="bg-neutral-50 dark:bg-neutral-900/40 p-4 rounded-lg">
          <dt class="font-bold text-neutral-900 dark:text-white">Can I compare non-JSON text using this tool?</dt>
          <dd class="text-neutral-600 dark:text-neutral-400 mt-1.5 leading-relaxed">
            Yes. If either input is invalid JSON, the comparator bypasses the parsing engine and defaults to standard raw text line-by-line diffing. However, for dedicated multi-line string and document comparisons, you will get a better experience using our dedicated <a href="/text-diff" class="text-emerald-600 dark:text-emerald-400 underline hover:text-emerald-700">Text Diff Tool</a>.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-neutral-50 dark:bg-neutral-900/40 p-4 rounded-lg">
          <dt class="font-bold text-neutral-900 dark:text-white">Does the order of JSON keys matter?</dt>
          <dd class="text-neutral-600 dark:text-neutral-400 mt-1.5 leading-relaxed">
            Standard JSON formatting aligns object keys exactly as they are written. If keys are shuffled between payloads, the diff engine highlights the reordered lines as removals and additions to ensure no subtle structural modifications go unnoticed during code reviews.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </section>

  </article>
</ToolLayout>